<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikola Pašić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikola Pašić was a Serbian statesman and a founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-radical-party">People's Radical Party</a> who served many times as prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. He led Serbia through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfu-declaration">Corfu Declaration</a> of 1917 on a union of the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. In the new kingdom he championed a centralised state dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic.jpg" alt="Nikola Pašić" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NikolaPasic--balkancockpitpol00pric_0191.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikola Pašić was born in Zaječar in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on 18 December 1845. Accounts of his family origins differ: Serbian and Slovenian writers place his ancestors in the Tetovo region or at Veliki Izvor near Zaječar, while some Bulgarian writers claimed a Bulgarian origin, which Serbian authors refuted. He studied engineering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and became politically active at the Polytechnical School in Zürich, among Serbian students who later formed the core of the socialist and radical movement, including Svetozar Marković.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pašić became leader of the movement after Marković died in 1875 and was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> in 1878. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-radical-party">People's Radical Party</a>, the first systematically organised Serbian party, was formally established in January 1881 with Pašić as its first president. After the Timok Rebellion against King Milan I he was sentenced to death in absentia, fled, and spent six years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> until a pardon in 1889. He was then president of the National Assembly and mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, and became prime minister for the first time in 1891.</p><p>After the murder of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> in 1903 the Radicals won the elections of 4 October and stayed in almost uninterrupted power for fifteen years. Pašić led governments during the customs war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> that began in 1906 and was a major player in forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-league">Balkan League</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> almost doubled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s territory. After the Assassination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> on 28 June 1914, historians disagree about how much he knew beforehand. He led the government in exile on Corfu, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfu-declaration">Corfu Declaration</a> paved the way for a South Slav state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> was proclaimed on 1 December 1918, and Pašić was regarded as its de facto prime minister, but Prince-regent Alexander asked Stojan Protić to form the government and Pašić stepped down on 20 December. He represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> and was prime minister again from 1921 to July 1924 and from November 1924 to 1926. Both he and Alexander wanted a unitary state, he because he feared a federal one would fall apart and that Serbs could be outvoted. During his last term the kingdom's first constitution was created. He died of a heart attack on 10 December 1926. A square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> bears his name and holds his statue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikola Pašić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…6 March 1882 until his abdication on 6 March 1889, after the lost war and the total electoral victory of the People's Radical Party, which under Sava Grujić and <b>Nikola Pašić</b> had come to power in 1888 with a new constitution based on the liberal Constitution of Belgium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Corfu Declaration of July 1917 the Serbian prime minister <b>Nikola Pašić</b> and Trumbić agreed on a single kingdom under the Serbian Karađorđević dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Veliki Izvor, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.92,22.33&amp;year=1897" aria-label="Veliki Izvor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5086 608 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nikola Pašić"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5536 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5536 878h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.92,22.33&amp;year=1897">What was here in 1897? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
